  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{\u2033}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-5$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5x}$ and $x{e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}$ is not a solution.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
